      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Joint Probability From a Table",
      "problem": "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P((X,Y)\\in \\{(1,4),(1,6),(2,6)\\})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above.",
      "worked_solution": "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in \\{(1,4),(1,6),(2,6)\\}) & =P((X,Y)=(1,4)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,6)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(1,4)+f(1,6)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.25+0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
